# rust-docs — AGENTS.md

## Domain

This project studies the Rust programming language from the official Rust Book. The study covers ownership, traits, concurrency, and tooling. The official Rust Book (doc.rust-lang.org/book) serves as the canonical source.

## Precedence chain — obligatory

The Learning Precedence Chain governs all work. Each layer completes before the next layer advances.

`precept/` → `concept/` → `reference/` → `fixture/`

`script/` runs parallel — automation and registry population serve any layer.

Precepts state the rule. Concepts organize the study. Reference distills the verified truth. Fixtures practice the material. Work follows this order strictly.

### Directory roles

Precept files govern all work. Each precept file carries one declarative rule per action-domain. Precept files use the `{action}-{domain}.md` naming form, for example `prefer-official-reference.md`.

Concept files organize the book into study anchors. Each concept file divides one topic into sub-concepts. Every concept arising from the study gets written — none skipped. Concept files use the `{topic}.md` naming form, for example `ownership.md`.

Reference files distill conventions and citations from the official book. Each reference file carries verbatim quotes with claim mapping. Reference governs fixtures. Reference files use the `{name}.md` naming form, for example `conventions.md`.

Fixture files hold practice code per book chapter. Each fixture file contains raw learning code. Reference governs fixtures. Fixture files use the `ch{NN}-{topic}.rs` naming form, for example `ch04-ownership.rs`.

Script files provide automation and registry population. Each script file uses the `{action}-{subject}.rb` naming form, for example `push-registry.rb`. `run-logged.sh` logs any command into `_knowledge/_bitacora/task-stdout/` — `bash run-logged.sh {name} -- {command}`.

## Layer semantics (governing rule)

```text
concept/    EVERY concept arising from study gets written — none skipped
reference/  CITATIONS from the official book — verbatim quotes, claim mapping
fixture/    compilable practice code — rustc must accept each file
```

## Toolchain

Rust toolchain: rustc 1.97.1. Fixtures compile with `rustc --edition 2021 {file}.rs`.

## Anchored skills

`skills/` holds a symlinked skill catalog. The catalog links live to the sources — no copies, no staleness. Twelve symlinks resolve 30 SKILL.md files:

```text
workspace (relative → ../../../.opencode/skills/): knowledge-languages, playwright-dispatcher,
    reason-invariants, reason-quantitative, reason-verbal, refactor-skill, search-dispatcher,
    semantic-dispatcher, study-foundations, workflow-dispatcher
home (absolute): find-skills → ~/.agents/skills/find-skills, omarchy → ~/.claude/skills/omarchy
```

### Semantic (entity context)

`semantic-dispatcher` routes semantic MCP tasks: `use-semantic-search` (find patlib entities by meaning), `use-semantic-stats` (embedding counts), `use-semantic-drift` (patlib/vector consistency), `use-semantic-embed` (upsert vectors), `use-semantic-purge` (stale rows), `use-semantic-eval` (MRR/Recall/NDCG).

### Search (web research)

`search-dispatcher` routes web-search MCP tasks: `use-exa` (semantic search + clean extraction), `use-parallel-search` (multi-query search + fetch).

### Playwright (browser)

`playwright-dispatcher` routes browser automation: `use-playwright-core` (navigation, clicks, snapshots), `use-playwright-ai-mode` (Google AI Mode), `use-playwright-debug` (traces, assertions), `use-playwright-network-storage` (mocking, auth), `use-playwright-vision` (vision-mode coordinates).

### Reasoning (analysis)

`reason-quantitative` encodes situations into quantitative structure. `reason-verbal` surveys and structures verbal evidence. `reason-invariants` validates invariant-bearing layers.

### Workflow (bookkeeping + scripting)

`workflow-dispatcher` routes workflow tasks: `bitacora-workflow` (todo → log → report), `manage-bash-flows` (atomic bash scripts, orchestrators), `structure-stdout` (pipe contracts), `survey-scripts` (survey workflow), `use-context-seven` (library docs).

### Study + language

`study-foundations` runs the learning pipeline for uncharted topics (foundations → decompose → research). `knowledge-languages` dispatches language role/ring per `ref/{language}.md`.

### Home + ecosystem

`find-skills` discovers and installs skills. `omarchy` configures the desktop/system (hypr, waybar, walker, terminals). `refactor-skill` rewrites skills to the canonical format.

## Ring topology — project

Every folder in this project belongs to one of six ordinal rings by role. Ring order governs dependency. Inner rings serve outer rings; outer rings never call inward.

```text
R0  Scripts     script/             automation — run-logged.sh, registry population; serves every ring
R1  Bitacora    _knowledge/_bitacora/  session records — task-stdout, task-report, task-todo; shared substrate
R2  Precept     precept/            governing rules — declarative action-domain files
R3  Concept     concept/            study anchors — topic division, one file per chapter
R4  Reference   reference/          distilled truth — citations with claim mapping
R5  Fixture     fixture/            practice material — compilable per-chapter code
```

Support layers sit outside the ordinal chain:

```text
rings/    mirrored ring-topology specifications — read-only references
skills/   symlinked skill catalog — live links to sources, never stale
```

Ring constraints:

- Scripts serve every ring; bitacora records every ring. Inner rings complete before outer rings advance (precept → concept → reference → fixture).
- Fixture compiles against reference claims; reference distills from concept anchors; concept derives from precept rules.
- Support layers inform the chain; they never govern it.

## Ring specifications

`rings/` holds ring-topology specifications mirrored from `.opencode/entities/specifications/`:

```text
SPEC.KNOWLEDGE.CLASSIFICATION.TOPOLOGY.md   — knowledge ring topology (governing)
SPEC.CODE.RING.TOPOLOGY.md                  — code ring topology
SPEC.DIRECTORY.RING.TOPOLOGY.md             — directory ring topology
SPEC.LANGUAGE.RING.TOPOLOGY.md              — language ring topology
SPEC.CODE.ELEMENT.NAME.md                   — element naming spec
```

## Sources

The official Rust Book (doc.rust-lang.org/book) serves as the canonical reference for all claims. The archived rust-coding study (`.archive/rust-coding/`) holds prior glossary terms — cross-reference the terms, write new text.

## Delegation

Root provides patterns, terms, and shared substrate. This project owns the Rust Book study.
